  fl(["Mughal: Khudkasht + patta", "1765 Diwani, Dual Govt", "1772 Farming System", "1793 Permanent Settlement", "1801/1820 Ryotwari", "1822/1833 Mahalwari", "Cash crops, famine", "1859 / 1885 Acts"], "At a glance: the route", 4),
  bx('pk', "<b>Teacher's FIVE-QUESTION FRAME</b> (learn every system as a row): <b>1 Landowner? 2 Assessment? 3 Settlement with? 4 Collection by? 5 Tenants' rights?</b> Why it matters: State's duty = economic security; in a pre-industrial country that = agrarian economy (agriculture big share of GDP, ryots big share of work profile)."),

  sec("MUGHAL BLUEPRINT (the yardstick)", 'b'),
  t("<b>Multiple right holders</b>", "<b>State (King)</b>: right to receive, decide, revise LR; <b>khalisa</b> LR goes straight up; from <b>jagirs</b> to <b>Jagirdars</b> (right to receive LR, by State grant)", "<b>Zamindar</b>: <b>Vatan right</b> to collect LR + keep <b>10% commission</b>; also police duties; <b>NOT owner</b>", "<b>Khudkasht</b> (actual cultivator): customary occupancy right (cannot be thrown off) + right to fair LR via <b>Patta system</b>; also pays <b>abwabs</b> (informal tribute) to Primary Zamindar", w=22),
  fl(["Khudkasht (patta, abwabs)", "Primary Zamindar (commission)", "Intermediary Zamindar (cut)", "Jagirdar (jagir) or State (khalisa)"], "Mughal revenue chain", 4),
  bx('bl', "<b>Patta</b> = document of what the ryot owes (who cultivates, plot, crop, LR demand) = legal safeguard vs over-extraction (a rent agreement). <b>Amils and Amalguzars</b> (govt officers) supervised so Zamindars did not under-report. <b>Legal private ownership = British idea</b> (English law). Land not a commodity (only conquest or gift): no sale, lease, mortgage, no land market; <b>land market begins 1793</b>. Pre-British, 'rent' and 'revenue' used interchangeably."),
  bx('ye', "<b>Handout figure slip:</b> bottom box says 'Amlas'; text names State supervisors as <b>Amils and Amalguzars</b>. Amlas = private employees of Zamindars, not State officers. Use Amils and Amalguzars."),
  bx('bl', "<b>Ijaradari</b> = State in a hurry for money auctioned the right to collect LR to the highest bidder (British replicate it 1772)."),
  tb(["Five questions", "Mughal", "Ijaradari (fallback)"], ["Landowner", "Khudkasht (practically his milkiyat; legally only the King)", "Ryot or Khudkasht (practical)"], ["Assessment", "Detailed survey; % of gross produce (e.g. one-third); revisable", "Summary, by auction; an amount"], ["Settlement with", "Ryot (gives <b>Qubuliyat</b> to State; State gives patta)", "The Ijaradar, via auction"], ["Collection by", "Non-State actor: Zamindar", "The Ijaradar"], ["Tenants", "Rightless, landless <b>Pahikashts</b>", "(not written)"]),

  sec("RIGHTS, ASSESSMENT, SETTLEMENT (primer)", 'g'),
  tb(["Layer of rights in land", "Right"], ["a", "Ryot: cultivate and occupy plot"], ["b", "Fair rent (tenant) or fair LR (owner)"], ["c", "Owner: sell, lease, rent, mortgage, receive proceeds"], ["d", "Collect taxes (State or Vatan holders e.g. Zamindars)"], ["e", "Receive proceeds and revise rates (State; Jagirdars by State grant)"]),
  bx('bl', "Two kinds of rights: <b>given by law</b> (State rules) and <b>customary</b> (society's conventions, e.g. Vatan rights of Zamindars). <b>Assessment</b> = how much tax: by detailed <b>field survey</b> (soil quality, irrigation, crop type, plot size, rainfall; board adds drought/flood frequency, past LR trends) or <b>summary</b> (rough); a <b>fixed absolute amount</b> or <b>% of produce</b>; <b>permanent</b> or <b>revisable</b>. <b>Settlement</b> = with whom the State negotiates and <b>who deposits</b> LR: with cultivator (Ryotwari-style) or intermediary (Zamindari, Mahalwari)."),
  bx('bl', "Rent = always paid by a tenant to an owner; taxes = always borne by the owner; lease = long-term rent; salary = employees only. State is ultimate recipient, may collect via officers or non-State intermediaries. GST analogy: shopkeeper deposits tax the buyer bears. In land systems <b>owner always bears tax</b>; settlement may or may not be with the owner: PS 1793 landowner (Zamindar); Ryotwari 1801, 1820 landowner (ryot); Mahalwari 1822 <b>not</b> the landowner (the Mahal via Lambardar)."),
  bx('pk', "<b>Zamindar changes meaning:</b> before 1793 = LR collector, not owner; from 1793 = legal landowner ('conditional ownership': Sunset Clause can take land); after 1947 'Zamindari abolition' = abolishing big landownership."),

  sec("EXPERIMENT 1: DUAL GOVERNMENT 1765-72", 'r'),
  t("<b>1765 Diwani</b>", "Treaty of Allahabad (ToA 1765): Diwani of <b>Bengal, Orissa, Bihar</b>", "EIC appointed <b>Deputy Subadar Muhammad Reza Khan</b> as its Diwan; Mughal collection continued = <b>Orientalism</b> (Indian traditions best run on their own terms)", "Greed anyway: frantic squeezing, <b>Patta ignored</b>, rents far beyond pattas, then <b>back-to-back droughts</b>", "<b>Great Bengal Famine 1770</b>: <b>50 lakh to 1 crore</b> dead; EIC <b>doubled demand in 1770</b>, used <b>brutality</b>; first British whistleblowers wrote to London papers", "<b>Reza Khan scapegoat</b>: jailed <b>without trial 1772-74</b>", w=22),
  bx('ye', "<b>Famine toll:</b> this handout 50 lakh to 1 crore; Ch 10 handout about 1 crore, one-third of Bengal. Quote the range; 'about one crore' = upper end."),
  bx('pk', "<b>Teacher's stressed phrases:</b> Deputy Subadar, Orientalism, Patta ignored, back-to-back droughts, doubled in 1770, brutality, imprisoned without trial, 1772-74. Famine = <b>nature</b> factor (droughts) + <b>human</b> factor (doubled demand, ignored pattas, brutality) (Ch 10)."),
  t("<b>1772 bankruptcy</b>", "Since 1757 EIC bought Indian goods with <b>India's own LR</b> (not English gold): famine hit income directly (Drain logic, Ch 10); LR collapsed", "Causes: officials' corruption (<b>Nabobs</b>), 1770 famine, heavy army + Dual System costs", "Bailout from Parliament: <b>1.4 million pounds at 4%</b>, strict condition of oversight = <b>Regulating Act 1773</b> (Ch 17)", w=20),

  sec("EXPERIMENT 2: FARMING SYSTEM 1772", 'b'),
  t("<b>Warren Hastings 73-85</b> (Orientalist)", "Revived Mughal <b>Ijaradari</b> = <b>Farming System</b>; Revenue Farmer = Ijaradar (like a farmer extracting profit by contract, not a tiller)", "District right to collect LR <b>auctioned to highest bidder</b>; worked under a <b>European DC</b> (English, Scottish or Irish, not English alone)", "Farmers squeezed max to recover bids + profit; demand so high that <b>peasants paid nothing: self-defeating</b>; revenue did not improve, rural population ruined", "<b>Assessment = auction; settlement = Revenue Farmer</b>", w=22),
  bx('pk', "<b>Ryot's three blows in a row:</b> 1757+ high LR, <b>1770</b> Great Bengal Famine, <b>1772</b> Ijaradari. Till 1813 ideology = <b>Orientalism</b> ('Rule India the India way'): Mughal collection kept after 1765, Ijaradari revived 1772; turn away after <b>Charter Act 1813</b> (Ch 17). Hastings 73-85 years as the board lists: 'To Purandhar 1776 @ Ma', 'To Salbai 1782', 'HA 61-82: military alliance 1769', '2nd A-My 1780-84' (To = Treaty, Ma = Marathas, HA = Haidar Ali 61-82; Ch 12, 13)."),

  sec("EXPERIMENT 3: PERMANENT SETTLEMENT ACT 1793", 'r'),
  mn('BOB', "<b>B</b>engal · <b>O</b>rissa · <b>B</b>ihar <i>(+ Benaras + North Madras)</i> = where PS 1793 applied (teacher's 'BOB + Benaras + N. Madras')"),
  t("<b>Cornwallis 86-93</b> (GG of Bengal 1786-93; 1786-1793)", "Sent with <b>one assignment: fix the LR problem</b>; passed PS through GG(Bengal)'s Council, <b>extended to Madras</b> on his instructions", "<b>AUTHORITARIAN</b> ('my way or highway'); from a landlord family, inspired by English <b>agrarian capitalism</b>; = <b>Believer</b> in English landlordism", "<b>By 1801 Thomas Munro</b> convinced Court of Directors to replace it with Ryotwari in Madras; PS survived in <b>North Madras</b>", w=22),
  tb(["Five questions", "PS 1793"], ["Landowner", "The Zamindar"], ["Assessment", "No field survey; summary; absolute amount (not %); not revisable"], ["Settlement with", "The Zamindar"], ["Collection by", "District Collector (DC)"], ["Tenants", "No rights: Zamindar may charge any rent and remove them"]),
  t("<b>Pillar 1: land to Zamindar</b>", "<b>Full proprietary rights first time</b> = legal owners; land a <b>commodity</b> (sold, bought, leased, rented, mortgaged); hereditary", "<b>Why:</b> (a) <b>ideology</b>: English landlordism, make Zamindars <b>agrarian capitalists</b>; security of property induces investment (b) agrarian capitalism = food security, agri trade, more EIC LR (c) <b>loyalty</b> of traditional rural leaders = political stability", "<b>Revenue Farmers</b> not made owners: outsiders to the agrarian economy, no expertise", "Board: a big landowner invests (irrigation, machines as in Industrial Revolution 1750-1850, hired labour); candidates = rich Zamindar (<b>by 1727, 15 Intermediary Zamindars collected 50% of Bengal Suba's LR</b>, group of <b>Murshid Quli Khan (MQK 17-27)</b>), moneylender, urban merchant; Zamindar thinks of agriculture 24x7 (commission), Ijaradars think margin and interest", w=26),
  bx('pk', "<b>'PS 1793 = DnR' (Divide and Rule)</b>: leaders and followers; DnR can pit leader vs leader, follower vs follower, or favour one tier (L happy F sad, or L sad F happy; e.g. 'Bangladesh, Nepal'). Board does not state which pattern for PS. Read from Impact: leaders (Zamindars) rewarded, masses (ryots) stripped of rights (a reading, not a board statement)."),
  t("<b>Pillar 2: settlement with Zamindar</b>", "Fewer, bigger counterparties ('LR payers'): simpler collection", "Zamindar responsible for <b>depositing LR</b>", w=20),
  t("<b>Pillar 3: permanent assessment</b>", "<b>10/11</b> of the Zamindar's pre-existing revenue (collected from ryots <b>before deducting his commission</b>), <b>base year 1789-90</b>", "<b>Fixed forever, absolute annual amount, not %</b> ('FIG not %')", "Why permanent + absolute: (a) <b>incentivise Zamindar</b>: no rise however profit grew (b) <b>certainty</b> even in drought (c) <b>less administration</b>, no repeat surveys, less corruption (d) <b>trade-off</b>: State gave up future increases so set initial demand <b>very high</b> (inflation would shrink it to 'practically nothing in 100 years')", "Estimates: some say <b>double the 1765 level</b>; others <b>20% above 1757 levels</b>", w=24),
  tb(["Board arithmetic", "Rs"], ["Ryot's produce", "100"], ["Before 1793: LR = two-thirds = 66%, collected by Zamindar", "66; ryot keeps 34"], ["Zamindar keeps 10% of 66", "6.6; State gets <b>59.4</b>"], ["After 1793: 10/11 x 66", "<b>60 fixed forever</b>"], ["Above 60 is Zamindar's", "e.g. Zamindar takes 13, ryot keeps 40 - 13 = 27"], ["Produce 120 in 1794", "State still 60; Zamindar keeps gain, invests"], ["Produce 0 in 1795", "State still 60; Zamindar carries risk"]),
  t("<b>Pillar 4: SUNSET CLAUSE</b>", "No LR by <b>evening of pre-set deadline</b> = land <b>auctioned to anyone who agrees to pay the LR</b> (buyer only has to agree, not pay in full)", "Buyer bids for the <b>LR, not land value</b>: e.g. agrees to pay Rs 60 a year ('who can pay the value of 300 villages? No one': why workable)", "Collection by State officers: <b>District Collector</b>", w=22),
  bx('pk', "<b>Prelims 2024 (b) 2 only</b> " + P('2024') + ": (1) F: Ryotwari is not Cornwallis 86-93's, and bad harvest earns <b>remission</b> (reduction), not <b>exemption</b> (zero). (2) T: defaulting Zamindar loses land = Sunset Clause."),

  sec("IMPACT OF PS 1793 (7 points)", 'b'),
  t("<b>Impact</b>", "<b>1 Short term:</b> frequent land <b>auctions</b> (demand too high, defaults); buyers <b>Jotedars</b> (rich farmers, less wealthy than Zamindars) + Zamindar's own family; later <b>stabilised</b>, Zamindars <b>loyal</b> (<b>1857</b> Bengal's Zamindars mostly stayed British)", "<b>2 Long term:</b> British got <b>certainty and regularity</b> of LR (achieved)", "<b>3 Vision failed:</b> Zamindars <b>did not invest</b> (little surplus, would not risk old wealth)", "<b>4 Absentee landlordism:</b> leased lands to intermediaries for a lease amount, moved to cities", "<b>5 Ryot catastrophic:</b> PS gave rights to Zamindars, <b>not ryot's customary rights</b>: <b>tenant at will</b>, evicted without protection; patta ignored, high rents, <b>abwabs</b> now illegal yet still collected", w=26),
  t("<b>6 Regulation VII of 1799</b>", "Cornwallis 86-93 had removed Zamindars' police powers (a businessman, not law enforcement)", "<b>Wellesley 98-05</b> reversed: Zamindar could (a) <b>seize</b> tenant property (crops, cattle) without court order on default (b) <b>imprison</b> tenants (c) <b>increase rents at will</b>", "Class slide: <b>wave of peasant revolts after 1793</b>", w=22),
  t("<b>7 Litigation rose: Cornwallis 86-93 Code of 1793</b> " + P('2011'), "(a) <b>DC stripped of all judicial and magisterial powers</b>: revenue disputes once settled swiftly on the spot now formally filed in <b>Diwani Adalats</b>", "(b) <b>court fees abolished</b> ('accessible' justice): flood of genuine + frivolous suits; <b>reimposed 1795</b>", "(c) <b>Vakils</b> (lawyers) formally recognised + rigid hierarchy of appeals: cases deliberately prolonged", "Judicial reforms belong to Ch 18; here because of Prelims 2011", w=24),
  bx('pk', "<b>Prelims 2011: (d) None of these</b>: (a) strong Zamindars did not mean more suits: ryots had no means to sue; (b) EIC already overlord of the region since 1765 (Diwani); (c) courts became less efficient. Key phrase 'Cornwallis 86-93 Code of 1793'."),
  bx('pk', "<b>Prelims 2001 (b) no official check upon the Zamindar</b> " + P('2001') + ": slide: <b>Bengal Regulation VIII of 1793</b> required Zamindars to issue <b>pattas</b> (rent + tenancy terms); ryot could demand one. Gap = enforcement: govt cared about punctual payment. (a) trust, (c) pattas = Zamindar's duty, (d) protected cultivators: not the reason."),
  bx('gr', "<b>Mains FYQ 'empire BUILT and SUSTAINED by DnR'</b>: <b>Built</b> = created division (Triple Alliance, Treaty of Amritsar 1809, Subsidiary Alliance taking external affairs) + used division (factionalism, e.g. Treaty of Bassein 1802, SA with Peshwa Baji Rao II 96-18). <b>Sustained = PS 1793</b> (loyal Zamindars). Ch 11, 13, 6."),
  t("<b>Rent Act 1859 (Bengal)</b>", "After decades of peasant revolts. (a) tenant on same plot <b>over 12 years</b>: occupancy rights, no eviction without court order, no rent rise without just cause (b) <b>under 12 years</b>: rents not raised frequently (c) <b>Patta system revived</b>: rents at recorded rates (d) Zamindars <b>lose power to imprison</b>", "Board: 'No more tenant at will'. <b>Poor implementation:</b> evict just before 12 years; Zamindars kept <b>lathiyals</b> (club-men, bribery); officials blind", "Failure drove <b>Indigo Revolt (1859-63)</b> and <b>Pabna Agrarian Leagues (1873)</b>: ryots fought for occupancy rights (Ch 20, 22)", w=24),
  t("<b>Bengal Tenancy Act 1885 (Act III of 1885)</b>", "Followed <b>Ripon 80-84's Rent Commission 1880</b>; passed under <b>Dufferin 84-88</b>; enacted by <b>Bengal Legislative Council</b>", "Rights of Zamindars and ryots clearly defined; <b>Zamindar powers reduced</b>", "<b>Occupancy strengthened:</b> tenants on <b>different plots in same village totalling 12+ years</b> get occupancy over the <b>last plot</b> = loophole closed", "Implemented <b>sincerely</b> this time", w=24),

  sec("RYOTWARI SETTLEMENT", 'b'),
  mn('SCAM-B', "<b>S</b>ind · <b>C</b>oorg · <b>A</b>ssam · <b>M</b>adras · <b>B</b>ombay = Ryotwari areas (teacher's 'SCAM-B')"),
  t("<b>Ryotwari followed annexations</b>", "<b>Sind 1843</b> (Charles Napier)", "<b>Coorg 1834</b> (coffee plantations)", "<b>Assam 1824-26</b> (First Anglo-Burmese War; Yandabo 1826)", "<b>Bombay 1817-19</b> (Third Anglo-Maratha War)", "<b>Madras</b> in pieces: 1766 Northern Sarkars; 3rd + 4th Anglo-Mysore Wars (Malabar + Baramahal from 3rd, Kanara from 4th, Dindigul too); <b>1801 the Carnatic</b> (Ch 12)", w=22),
  bx('ye', "<b>Berar:</b> class map lists Berar under Ryotwari; handout list does not. Both agree on the five places above."),
  t("<b>Madras: from PS to Munro</b>", "First attempt = same as Bengal: extend PS; <b>Cornwallis 86-93 and Wellesley 98-05</b> tried to create big Zamindars by recognising <b>Poligars</b> (landed magnates with private armies, autonomous since <b>Vijayanagar 1336-1646</b>): <b>failed</b>", "<b>1792</b> experiment under <b>Alexander Read</b> in <b>Baramahal</b> (annexed in Third Anglo-Mysore War 1790-92); <b>1801 Munro</b> convinced Court of Directors; <b>1801-07</b> applied in Madras", "<b>Diluted after Munro left for Britain 1807</b>; re-established firmly as <b>Governor of Madras 1820-27</b>; he died in India", w=24),
  bx('pk', "<b>Prelims 2017 (c) 2 and 3 only</b> " + P('2017') + ": Cornwallis 86-93 = PS 1793 architect (even tried PS in Madras via Poligars). <b>Read 1792, Munro 1801-07 and 1820</b> = Ryotwari pair. Hook: Read + Munro = Ryotwari (Scottish, yeoman); Cornwallis 86-93 = Permanent (English landlordism)."),
  tb(["Why Munro chose Ryotwari (5)", "Reason"], ["a Scottish ideology (<b>stressed</b>)", "Scotland's hero = <b>Yeoman farmer</b> (landowner who cultivates, a richer Khudkasht); as Orientalist argued ancient India had no intermediaries between State and cultivator"], ["b No big Zamindars", "MQK 17-27 made super-rich Intermediary Zamindars (15 collect 50% by 1727) so PS viable in Bengal; Madras had none"], ["c State finances", "Madras stressed by low productivity + wars: <b>three Carnatic Wars (1746-63)</b>, <b>four Anglo-Mysore Wars (1767-99)</b>; PS fixes LR forever, no room to grow income"], ["d Eliminate intermediaries", "Poligars' profits at State's expense (slide adds Deshmukhs): share to State + lighter ryot burden"], ["e Political stability", "Remove Poligars as powerful non-State actors; masses' loyalty by giving land (board: 'To bar POLITICAL AUTHORITY': private armies + autonomy)"]),
  tb(["Five questions", "Ryotwari"], ["Landowner", "The ryot, with legal title"], ["Settlement", "Directly with ryot, individually"], ["Assessment", "Detailed field surveys (soil productivity, irrigation, acreage, crops, past trends), <b>in %, revisable</b>, remission in bad harvest"], ["Collection", "State officers: DC and his juniors (Indians)"], ["Tenants", "(not stated on slides)"]),
  bx('pk', "<b>Field survey = crux of Ryotwari</b> (very tough). Improper survey = unjust LR = landlessness. Three roads to land loss: abandon land; default then govt auction; moneylender interest, default, lose mortgaged land. Board: <b>learn surveyors' names</b>; 'Bombay: Wingate Settlement = Ryotwari'."),
  tb(["", "Phase 1: gross produce", "Phase 2: rental value / net produce"], ["Where, when", "Madras: <b>Read + Munro 1792-1820</b>", "Bombay (<b>Pringle 1830, Wingate 1835</b>), Madras (<b>1855, 1864</b>)"], ["Demand", "<b>33% to 50%</b> of estimated gross produce", "Net produce (gross value minus seeds, manure, bullocks, labour, implements): <b>Ricardian Economic Rent</b>"], ["Flaw / fix", "Improper surveys; arbitrary; ignored cost of cultivation", "Reformed after ruin of agrarian economy"], ["Result", "Severe over-assessment, abandonment ('Kheti hi chhod diye'); failure, borrow at high interest, default, lose land", "Bombay: Wingate fixes; Madras: capped at <b>50%</b>"]),
  t("<b>Phase 2 Bombay</b>", "<b>Elphinstone</b> brought Ryotwari (slide: <b>1819</b>); <b>no fresh survey</b>, used existing <b>Maratha records</b>", "<b>R.K. Pringle (1830)</b>: first applied <b>Economic Rent</b>; faulty calculation = excessive <b>55%</b>", "<b>Wingate Settlement 1835</b> (<b>Bombay Survey System</b>, Wingate + Goldsmid; across <b>Bombay Deccan by 1847</b>): fair LR, reasonable <b>annual amount for 30 years</b>", w=22),
  bx('ye', "<b>Pringle's date:</b> handout 1830; comparison slide 'PRINGLE 1830'; milestone slide 1824-1828 (survey years; 1824-28). Use <b>1830</b> for Economic Rent. <b>Wingate:</b> teacher notes 'check it was %' (handout says annual amount). Deccan splits into Bombay Deccan (Wingate) and Hyderabad Deccan."),
  t("<b>Phase 2 Madras</b>", "<b>Madras Torture Commission Report 1855</b>: severe oppression in collection", "<b>Madras Rules (1855/1864)</b>: LR capped at <b>50% of net produce / rental value for 30 years</b> (slide: Court of Directors ordered 30-year settlements with lower LR 1855; <b>1864 SoS Charles Wood</b> ordered half-of-net cap)", "Handout: this kept Madras <b>quiet in 1857</b>", "<b>Corruption:</b> lower bureaucracy relied on <b>Mirasidars</b> (rich landowner-cultivators as village revenue officers); DC + officers held concentrated <b>judicial, police, revenue</b> powers: Mirasidars oppressed ryots, forced defaults, <b>bought best lands in auctions</b>; goal of removing big intermediaries <b>failed</b>", w=24),
  bx('pk', "<b>Prelims 2012 (c) 1, 2, 3</b> " + P('2012') + " (handout and class agree): (1) rent paid directly by peasants to govt = settlement with ryot (2) pattas given = patta system preserved (3) surveyed and assessed before taxed = detailed field surveys."),
  t("<b>Impact in Bombay</b>", "After <b>3rd Anglo-Maratha War 1817-19</b> EIC first used <b>Patils + Deshmukhs</b> (nod to Orientalism), later officers", "<b>Patils lost status:</b> earlier executive, judicial, revenue, police powers + commission; now ordinary <b>salaried</b> officer", "<b>Massive debt</b> to <b>Marwari and Gujarati moneylenders</b>, central Deccan, <b>1850s</b>", "<b>Not solely Ryotwari:</b> crop failures + <b>cotton price collapse after US Civil War (1861-65)</b>: boom 1861-65 (Union naval blockade cut Confederate cotton), then US cotton flooded back, premium vanished", "= <b>Bombay Deccan Peasant Riots 1875</b>", w=24),

  sec("MAHALWARI SETTLEMENT", 'g'),
